{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 18, :comments ["<b>To judge</b> — to take the justice of the orphan and the crushed. And the word <b>terror</b>: <i>al ta'artzu</i> [do not be terrified], like <i>al tir'u</i> [do not fear]; and <i>aritz</i> [terrifying one] like <i>nora</i> [awesome]. And behold, <i>la'arot</i> enosh [to terrify man] = <i>liyro</i> enosh [to fear man]. For we find the word <i>yirah</i> both with mem, like <i>yir'u meHaShem</i> [they shall fear HaShem], and without mem, like <i>yir'ukha am shemesh</i> [those who fear you shall the sun]. Or the word <i>min ha'aretz</i> [from the earth] draws backward, like <i>odem pitdah v'bareqet</i> [sardius, topaz, and carbuncle]."], :book :psalms, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 10, :notes {0 "The phrase 'draws backward' apparently refers to a word at verse end that syntactically belongs to what precedes; the analogy to the gem list in Exodus 28:17 is obscure and possibly textually problematic."}, :source-witnesses [["Ibn Ezra on Psalms -- Daat" "http://www.daat.ac.il/he-il/tanach/parshanut_hamikra/full-text/eben-ezra/tehilim"] ["Wikisource Mikraot Gedolot" "http://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Mikraot_Gedolot/"] ["Tanakh, Venice, 1617-1619" "https://dlib.rsl.ru/viewer/01007514045#?page=4"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T19:27:01.310524504Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}